=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: false
faulty_devices: none
diagnosis_text:
The reported symptom — inability to reach pc_0_1 (10.0.1.2) from pc_0_0
(10.0.0.2) — does not exist in the live lab. Direct verification shows 0%
packet loss on a 5-packet ping and a clean 4-hop traceroute:
pc_0_0 → leaf_router_0_0 → spine_router_0_0 → leaf_router_0_1 → pc_0_1,
with round-trip times well under 4 ms.

Both spine routers are fully operational. All interfaces on spine_router_0_0
and spine_router_0_1 are in the "up" state. All eight eBGP sessions in the
fabric (leaf↔spine, spine↔super-spine) are established. Both leaf routers
hold two valid BGP paths for the remote-rack prefix — one via each spine —
and select spine_router_0_0 as the active next-hop purely because its BGP
router-ID (172.16.0.1) is numerically lower than spine_router_0_1's
(172.16.0.3); this is correct, deterministic BGP best-path behavior, not a
fault. spine_router_0_1 is a live, valid backup and would take over within
one BGP hold-timer expiry if spine_0_0 failed.

The super_spine's asymmetric prefix counts (0 received on spine_0_0,
2 on spine_0_1) are also expected: the super_spine's best path for both
prefixes traverses AS 65100, so spine_router_0_0 rejects those
advertisements via standard AS-path loop prevention. This has no operational
impact because both leaf routers learn the remote subnets directly from each
spine without involving the super_spine at all.

Root cause: there is no fault — the infra team's claim that a spine died is
incorrect. The network is healthy.

Corrective action: none required. If the user experienced a transient outage
earlier, it may have resolved on its own (e.g., a brief BGP session reset at
lab startup). No configuration changes are needed.
=== END ===
